Condo Residents app - versioning and release notes

Verified project configuration (source of truth):
- App display name: Matrix Condo
- Android namespace/application ID: com.asiamatrix.matrixcondo
- Android flavors: dev, prod (flavor dimension: environment)
  - dev: applicationIdSuffix = ".dev", versionNameSuffix = "-dev"
  - prod: no suffix
- iOS bundle ID: com.asiamatrix.matrixcondo
- iOS schemes/configurations verified: shared Runner scheme with Debug, Release, and Profile
- No iOS dev/prod flavors or schemes were found in the verified project
- pubspec.yaml version: 1.0.0+1
- android/app/build.gradle.kts uses:
  - versionCode = flutter.versionCode
  - versionName = flutter.versionName
- ios/Runner/Info.plist uses:
  - CFBundleShortVersionString = $(FLUTTER_BUILD_NAME)
  - CFBundleVersion = $(FLUTTER_BUILD_NUMBER)
- ios/Runner.xcodeproj/project.pbxproj Runner target uses:
  - CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION = "$(FLUTTER_BUILD_NUMBER)"
  - No MARKETING_VERSION setting was found for the Runner target
  - RunnerTests target has its own CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION = 1 and MARKETING_VERSION = 1.0; this is test-target only

Flutter version format:
- pubspec.yaml currently uses: version: 1.0.0+1
- 1.0.0 is the build name / marketing version shown to users
- 1 is the build number / build version used by stores for ordering
- --build-name and --build-number override those values for one build without changing pubspec.yaml
- Example: flutter build apk --flavor prod --release --build-name=1.0.1 --build-number=2

Android:
Verified flavor names: dev, prod

Production APK for device testing:
flutter build apk --flavor prod --release

Development APK for device testing:
flutter build apk --flavor dev --release

Production AAB for Google Play release:
flutter build appbundle --flavor prod --release

Development AAB when needed:
flutter build appbundle --flavor dev --release

Expected output paths:
- build/app/outputs/flutter-apk/app-prod-release.apk
- build/app/outputs/flutter-apk/app-dev-release.apk
- build/app/outputs/bundle/prodRelease/app-prod-release.aab
- build/app/outputs/bundle/devRelease/app-dev-release.aab

Google Play requires each uploaded release to use a versionCode higher than previously uploaded versions for that application.

iOS:
- CFBundleShortVersionString = $(FLUTTER_BUILD_NAME) means the user-facing version comes from Flutter's build name
- CFBundleVersion = $(FLUTTER_BUILD_NUMBER) means the build number comes from Flutter's build number
- FLUTTER_BUILD_NAME and FLUTTER_BUILD_NUMBER are provided through Flutter's generated iOS build configuration

Create an IPA with explicit version values:
flutter build ipa --release --build-name=1.0.1 --build-number=2

Alternative Xcode workflow:
1. Open ios/Runner.xcworkspace in Xcode
2. Select the Runner target
3. Use Product > Archive
4. Use the Archive Organizer to distribute the app

Verified Xcode configuration:
- Runner CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION is "$(FLUTTER_BUILD_NUMBER)"
- Runner Info.plist uses "$(FLUTTER_BUILD_NAME)" and "$(FLUTTER_BUILD_NUMBER)"
- The verified Runner target does not define MARKETING_VERSION

Do not assume manually changing Xcode Identity fields or Info.plist will override Flutter's build values. The verified configuration shows Flutter-provided values drive both iOS version fields. For explicit independent versions, prefer Flutter CLI flags.

Independent platform releases:
- iOS can receive a bug-fix release such as 1.0.1 with build number 2 without requiring a new Android release at the same time
- Android can remain on its previously released version until a new Android release is needed
- Each platform must maintain valid, increasing build numbers for its own distribution history
- Google Play versionCode and App Store build number are separate platform histories
- Always pass both --build-name and --build-number explicitly for releases
- Do not rely on pubspec.yaml defaults or Xcode settings for release versioning
- Do not reuse or decrease build numbers

Recommended simple approach:
- Keep pubspec.yaml as a baseline only
- Record the last released Android versionCode and iOS build number in this file or release notes
- Use explicit Flutter CLI flags for every release
- If independent version management later needs stronger automation, a future option is platform-specific version files or CI variables; that is not implemented in this project now

Existing useful commands:
flutter run --flavor prod --release
flutter run --flavor dev --release
flutter run --flavor dev --profile

flutter build apk --flavor dev --profile

// For app launcher icons
flutter pub run flutter_launcher_icons:main -f flutter_launcher_icons*

Shorebird release and OTA patch workflow is documented in the section below.
Shorebird release and OTA patch workflow:
Verified Shorebird configuration:
- shorebird.yaml exists with one app_id: f030e4d2-612d-4d62-9f4e-b7a71640fbee
- Shorebird flavors configured in shorebird.yaml: dev, prod
- Shorebird app IDs configured in shorebird.yaml:
  - dev: 0f0ec3d9-07d0-455e-9b97-d0e2ce247479
  - prod: 90c14d2f-2b5c-421e-985a-970b8c7c1a52
- Installed Shorebird CLI version: 1.6.130
- Android application IDs:
  - prod: com.asiamatrix.matrixcondo
  - dev: com.asiamatrix.matrixcondo.dev
- iOS bundle ID: com.asiamatrix.matrixcondo
- iOS Xcode project has no verified dev/prod flavors, build configurations, or schemes
- Android has verified dev and prod flavors

1. Version management:
- --build-name is the user-facing app version.
- --build-number is the build number.
- Google Play requires a new, valid version code for each new store release.
- Check the existing production release history before choosing version/build numbers.
- Dev and production flavors may have different application IDs and release histories.
- Do not reuse example version numbers blindly.

2. Android production release for Google Play:
Verified command pattern:
shorebird release android --flavor prod --build-name=<VERSION> --build-number=<BUILD_NUMBER>

The installed Shorebird CLI uses AAB as the default Android artifact type. A fixed local output path was not verified from the CLI help; use the path printed by the successful command.

Workflow:
1. Choose the correct production version and build number.
2. Run the Shorebird release command.
3. Verify that the release completed successfully and locate the generated .aab.
4. Test the release build.
5. Upload the .aab to the appropriate Google Play Console track.
6. Complete Google's review/release process as applicable.

Shorebird does not automatically publish the AAB to Google Play Console.

3. Android OTA patch workflow:
Verified command pattern for production:
shorebird patch android --flavor prod --release-version=<VERSION_OR_latest>

Use --release-version to target the intended Shorebird release. Use --release-version=latest only when the latest release is intentionally the target.

Notes:
- A patch targets an existing Shorebird release; verify the intended release before patching.
- Patches are intended for compatible Dart/Flutter code changes.
- Native Android code changes, plugin/native dependency changes, and other unsupported changes require a new store release.
- Do not assume that every change can be delivered through an OTA patch.
- Test the patch before relying on it in production.
- The installed CLI supports --allow-native-diffs, but it is not recommended because native changes cannot be included in a patch.

4. iOS release and patch workflow:
General release command pattern:
shorebird release ios --build-name=<VERSION> --build-number=<BUILD_NUMBER>

iOS release builds require macOS and Xcode. The generated IPA must still be submitted through the appropriate Apple distribution workflow; Shorebird does not automatically publish it to App Store Connect.

iOS patch command pattern supported by the installed CLI:
shorebird patch ios --release-version=<VERSION_OR_latest>

Caution:
- The project's Shorebird config declares dev and prod app IDs, but the iOS Xcode project has no verified dev/prod flavors, build configurations, or schemes.
- Verify flavor support before adding --flavor to an iOS Shorebird command.
- Use --release-version to target the intended iOS release.

5. When to use a store release versus a patch:
- New production release: create a Shorebird release and upload the generated artifact to the relevant app store.
- Compatible Dart-only fix: create a Shorebird patch for the intended existing release.
- Native code, native plugin, permissions, or Flutter SDK changes: determine whether a new store release is required; do not assume an OTA patch supports them.
- New version that must be reviewed/distributed through the store: create a new release and follow the store submission process.

6. Troubleshooting and safety:
- Verify the correct flavor and application ID.
- Verify the version and build number.
- Verify the correct Shorebird app/release.
- Verify the release artifact location and successful build output.
- Check signing and store validation errors.
- Check whether the change is eligible for a Shorebird patch.
- Use commands compatible with Shorebird CLI 1.6.130.
- Do not claim a command, option, artifact path, or project setting was verified unless it was actually inspected.
